.TH VIRTICLE 1 "" "virticle" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
virticle \- web controller for KVM/libvirt hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo virticle setup
[\fB\-\-admin\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-\-admin\-password\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-node\fR | \fB\-\-no\-node\fR] [\fB\-\-address\fR \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-firewall\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-undo\fR]
.br
.B sudo virticle node add
[\fIoptions\fR] [[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]]
.br
.B sudo virticle status
.br
.B sudo virticle node list
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B sudo virticle node remove
[\fB\-\-yes\fR] \fIname\fR
.br
.B virticle node prepare
[\fIoptions\fR] [[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]]
.br
.B virticle node unprepare
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]
.br
.B virticle backup
[\fB\-o\fR \fIfile|dir\fR] [\fB\-\-encrypt\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-\fR\fIrule\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B virticle restore
[\fB\-\-key\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] \fIfile\fR
.br
.B virticle web
.BR show | set | reset
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B virticle user
.BR reset\-2fa | reset\-password
\fIuser\fR
.br
.B virticle version
.br
.B virticle
[\fIflags\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Virticle is one program that serves a web UI and API and manages libvirt
nodes over SSH. Nothing but a small node command is installed on the managed
nodes. The controller keeps its users, node list, task log and audit log in a
SQLite database in the data directory.
.PP
The package runs it as the systemd service
.B virticle.service
as user
.BR virticle ,
serving HTTPS with a self-signed certificate on port 8463. The package does
not change the firewall and creates no administrator: run
.B sudo virticle setup
once. On one machine that is all; add more machines with
.BR "sudo virticle node add root@" \fIhost\fR.
.PP
The commands below except
.B version
work on the controller's data directory: run them as root or as the
controller's account. Each takes
.B \-\-data
.I dir
(default /var/lib/virticle on an installed machine). While the controller
runs they reach it through the socket control.sock in the data directory
(mode 0600; only root and the controller's account), so changes show at once
in the web UI. While it is stopped they work on the data directory themselves.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B virticle setup
Run once as root after installing. It chooses the web address and port (asked
on a terminal; Enter keeps all addresses and port 8463), checks that the port
is free, opens it in firewalld or ufw (asked on a terminal), makes sure the
service runs, creates the first administrator (asked on a terminal), and, when
this machine can run virtual machines, asks "Use this machine as a node too?"
and adds it. It ends with a summary (controller, administrator, nodes) and the
address to open. Running it again changes nothing and says what is done.
.B \-\-admin
.I name
and
.B \-\-admin\-password\-file
.I file
(the first line; or the variable VIRTICLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD) create the
administrator without questions.
.B \-\-node
and
.B \-\-no\-node
answer the node question; without a terminal and without them the machine is
not added.
.B \-\-address
.I addr
(an IP address of this machine, or
.BR all )
and
.B \-\-port
.I n
answer the address questions; they apply only while no administrator exists.
Without a terminal the port is opened, unless
.B \-\-no\-firewall
is given.
.B \-\-yes
asks nothing and answers yes.
.B \-\-check
changes nothing.
.B \-\-undo
removes the firewall rule that setup added (do this before uninstalling).
.TP
.B virticle node add \fR[[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR]
Prepare a host when it needs it and add it as a node, as Nodes > Add node does
in the web UI; without a host, this machine (run as root). The administrator
(root, or a user that may use sudo) signs in with the SSH agent,
.B \-\-identity
.IR file ,
or a password: asked on a terminal, or from
.B \-\-password\-file
.IR file ,
VIRTICLE_NODE_PASSWORD or
.BR \-\-password\-stdin .
It is used for this run only. A host that this controller's key already gets
into needs none.
.B \-\-root
lets the controller sign in as root instead of the service account
virticle\-node;
.B \-\-user
.I name
names the service account;
.B \-\-jump
.I user@host
reaches the host through a jump host;
.B \-\-take\-over
takes over a host that another Virticle controller manages (a terminal asks
instead).
.TP
.B virticle node list
The nodes, one line each: name, address and state. A node with an older
Virticle node command reads "Virticle update needed" and the command that
updates it.
.B \-\-json
prints JSON.
.TP
.B virticle node remove \fIname\fR
Remove a node (name: its host name, address or id) from the controller.
Nothing is uninstalled from it. A terminal asks first;
.B \-\-yes
does not.
.TP
.B virticle status
The controller's version, web address, service, administrator and every node
with its state, and for each problem the command that fixes it. Exit status 1
when something needs attention.
.TP
.B virticle node prepare \fR[[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR]
Prepare a host without adding it (what node add runs first; also how a node
gets this version's node command). Installs libvirt, QEMU, the guest disk
tools and the node command, sets up the access the controller needs and opens
the migration ports (TCP 49152 to 49215) in firewalld or ufw. Loads the KVM
module when it is missing and lists it in /etc/modules-load.d/virticle.conf.
It never changes the boot loader or the kernel command line and never reboots;
where a kernel argument is needed it names the exact arguments.
.B \-\-check
changes nothing. It takes the options of node add, and more; see
.BR "virticle node \-\-help" .
.TP
.B virticle node unprepare
Undo prepare: removes the account, key, node command, sudo rule, module list
and firewall rule. Packages, VMs, pools and networks stay.
.TP
.B virticle backup
Write the controller's own data (database, SSH key, certificates) to one file,
while the controller runs.
.B \-o
takes a file or a directory.
.B \-\-encrypt
asks for a passphrase (or reads it with
.BR \-\-passphrase\-file ).
With
.B \-o
DIR and one of
.BR \-\-keep\-last ,
.BR \-\-keep\-hourly ,
.BR \-\-keep\-daily ,
.BR \-\-keep\-weekly ,
.B \-\-keep\-monthly
N, older backups in DIR that the rules do not keep are deleted.
.TP
.B virticle restore
Put such a file back. The controller must be stopped. An existing data
directory is moved aside, never overwritten.
A scheduled backup made while backup encryption is on needs
.B \-\-key
FILE, the recovery key file, and its passphrase.
.TP
.B virticle web
Read (\fBshow\fR), change (\fBset\fR) or reset to plain HTTP on
127.0.0.1:8080 (\fBreset\fR) the stored web access settings, for a headless
install or a locked\-out administrator. Restart the controller afterwards.
.TP
.B virticle user
Recover a sign\-in from the controller's machine:
.B reset\-2fa
turns two\-step sign\-in off for a user,
.B reset\-password
sets a new password for a local account. No restart is needed.
.TP
.B virticle version
Print the version, commit and build date.
.TP
.B virticle
Run the controller (the service does this).
.SH OPTIONS
Run
.B virticle \-help
for the full list. The main flags of the controller are:
.TP
.BI \-listen " address"
Address to serve the web UI and API on. Given, it overrides the settings of
the Web access page. Without it, the stored settings decide; a new install
serves HTTPS with a self-signed certificate on port 8463, an installation
with users and no stored settings keeps 127.0.0.1:8080.
.TP
.BI \-data " dir"
Directory for the database and the controller's SSH key (default
/var/lib/virticle if it holds a database, else data).
.TP
.B \-no\-scheduler
Do not run scheduled snapshots and backups from this controller.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version, commit and build date and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VIRTICLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD
For scripts: the first administrator's password for setup.
.TP
.B VIRTICLE_NODE_PASSWORD
For scripts: the host administrator's password for node add, prepare and
unprepare.
.TP
.B VIRTICLE_JUMP_PASSWORD
Password for node jump hosts.
.TP
.B VIRTICLE_LISTEN
Default for \-listen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when a command failed or, for status, when something needs
attention; 2 for a wrong command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/virticle/virticle.env
Service environment: VIRTICLE_ARGS (extra flags) and VIRTICLE_JUMP_PASSWORD.
Kept across upgrades.
.TP
.I /var/lib/virticle
Data directory: virticle.db, id_ed25519, known_hosts, instance\-id, secret.key,
metrics.db, certificates, control.sock (the command line's socket while the
controller runs), and backups/ with the scheduled controller backups
(unless another folder is chosen under Settings > Controller backup). Copies of the database made before a schema
upgrade are named
.IR virticle.db.bak\-<version>\-<time> .
.SH EXAMPLES
One machine:
.PP
.nf
    sudo apt install virticle
    sudo virticle setup
.fi
.PP
From a script:
.PP
.nf
    virticle setup \-\-admin admin \-\-admin\-password\-file /root/admin.pw \-\-node
    virticle node add \-\-password\-file /root/node.pw root@192.0.2.11
    virticle status
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR /usr/share/doc/virticle/docs/install.md ,
.I /usr/share/doc/virticle/README.md
